[Music]  being in a shipyard in general sucks  without any of the coved stuff  madeleine walchko works on an american  container ship that got stuck in the  shanghai shipyard during the city's  covid lockdown and she documented her  two months-long experience once the  lockdown happened it was just kind of  one thing after the next  she and the crew had to rely on local  authorities for supplies we have a  delivery here of food and water took  regular cova tests  all while living on a semi-dismantle  ship the rainforest is back  we're just trying to keep busy just to  stay sane their struggle shows just how  hard it was for shipyards as well as  companies in the region to keep working  through shanghai's covid restrictions  [Music]  in february the president wilson docked  at a shipyard just north of the city's  port for repairs that were originally  meant to take a little over a month this  ship works 24 7. we don't stop weltchko  is the ship's second officer and is from  washington state she gave the wall  street journal permission to use her  footage  the plan was to finish the maintenance  then pick up containers at the port  watchko says when the work started some  days about 500 shipyard workers would  come aboard but about a month into their  stay shanghai entered a lockdown watchko  says the workers stopped coming and most  of the repair work ground to a halt  we're current kind of already used to  being on board the difference being that  we're not moving there's no work  progressing  restricted to the ship by local  authorities the crew was stuck on a  vessel that was midway through repairs  starting to get flooded already in here  unable to stock up on their regular food  supplies they relied on government  deliveries at the beginning of the  lockdown we had about a month meet left  it was  a little um terrifying the crew also had  to undergo regular covet testing  following a schedule set by the shipyard  authorities  the pcr test will be done on the 1st 2nd  4th 7th 10th 14th 17th and 21st days  watchko says all of this took a toll on  the mental health of the crew  it's difficult when there's no  set date you know no set plan  everyone's up and down all the time  the company cma cgm group which owns the  president wilson said they were in daily  contact with the crew to ensure their  health and safety and worked with local  authorities to organize food and other  necessities  about a month after things came to a  halt the shipyard implemented a system  that would allow workers to return to  the vessel despite the city's coveted  restrictions it's called a closed loop  basically the workers are locked down in  a confined area  and they're not allowed to leave  but they can still work  this close-off environment wasn't only  used at the shipyard but it's also been  implemented by factories and financial  companies where employees lived and  slept at work  some even setting up tents for privacy  it's a way for officials to balance two  competing goals curb the spread of covet  and keep production going to limit  economic damage and global supply chain  fallout  companies like tesla and china's largest  state-owned automaker saic all  implemented closed loops during  shanghai's lockdown  but this system isn't always running at  100 capacity compared to normal times  closed loop does not mean perfect it  doesn't mean perfect loop jared ward is  the chairman of the supply chain  committee at the american chamber of  commerce in shanghai he also works for  houston logistics a company that  organizes and manages the transport of  goods at the port for suppliers and  retailers you're basically concentrating  the labor without allowing them to go  home i mean there's a human toll to that  as well as just the general lack of  efficiency of having  enough people  many of the city shipyards also face  reduced capacity according to the  president wilson's crew only a fraction  of the original workforce returned to  the ship when work resumed it was just a  trickle like it was 10 people and then  it was 20 people and then it was 50 to  60 people along with fewer people there  were not enough materials coming into  shipyards in shanghai for repairs that's  because the lockdown created a logistics  headache truck drivers coming into the  city had to take covetus or quarantine  slowing down deliveries to factories and  the port even though you may have the  closest labor on site you still don't  have your components flowing into the  manufacturing site so with fewer people  and materials the ship's repairs dragged  on for months and the president wilson's  crew simply had to wait  we're never going to get out of here  the port of shanghai and the company  that owns a shipyard costco shipping  heavy industry didn't respond to  requests for comment the city's  government lifted covert restrictions on  june 1st and about a week later  the president wilson completed its  repairs and set out to pick up cargo at  the port we are finally on our way down  the yangtze river  but there's still uncertainty for  companies that rely on shipping and  manufacturing in the region biggest  concern is you know this coped situation  we went through in shanghai you know  easily could come back next month or you  know could come back in another location  and you know how is your supply chain  designed is it flexible enough to  actually deal with that  shipping analysts say as trucking and  activity at the port return to normal  they expect the resumption of export  shipments to have a ripple effect  creating congestion at other major ports  around the world especially as the back  to school and holiday shopping seasons  start to kick in companies operating in  china could become increasingly wary of  more disruptions caused by strickkovich  strategies that's why we see people  deciding about alternative supply points  whether they're still inside of china or  outside so this is where the impact will  be felt for i think many  certainly months but many years to come  the president wilson is finding its way  back into the global supply chain with  its final destination at the us's  busiest container poor complex at los  angeles and long beach  people don't really think about the  humans involved and that we have limits  just go go go all the time think about  the cargo and think about the money and  think about the ship when you're already  fatigued sleep deprived it's  excruciating at times  you

